# Compiled example/test binaries (no extension, root directory)
/simple
/multiple_contracts

# Compiled binaries dropped into a fixture crate root by an ad-hoc `rustc -o`
# run. A fixture crate's real bin targets are declared in its Cargo.toml and
# build into target/, so every legitimate tracked file sitting next to a
# fixture Cargo.toml has an extension — an extension-less one is build output.
# tests/fixtures/proof_assert/test_multi_field (a 439 KB Mach-O executable) was
# committed this way and reached the publication export as forbidden binary
# content. Ignore the whole class; subdirectories and any file with an
# extension are re-included on the two lines below.
tests/fixtures/*/*
!tests/fixtures/*/*/
!tests/fixtures/*/*.*

# ...but `!tests/fixtures/*/*/` above re-includes EVERY directory under a
# fixture, which cancels the blanket `target/` rule at the top of this file for
# exactly those paths. Running `trust-wp` (or `cargo trust-wp`) inside a fixture
# crate then leaves a couple of hundred megabytes of build output showing as
# untracked. Re-ignore it here, after the negations.
tests/fixtures/*/target/

# Rustc crash reports
rustc-ice-*.txt


# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Harness artifacts
target/creusot-replacement-review/
.claude/worktrees/
tests/creusot_compat/results-*.json
!tests/creusot_compat/results-should-fail.json
!tests/creusot_compat/results-examples.json

# Publish tool work area (allowlisted-snapshot export, staging clone, reports)
publish/.out/
target.noindex/
/target

# Harness fixture build dirs. These are BUILD ARTIFACTS and must never be
# untracked-but-visible: `tests/creusot_compat/harness_targets.py`
# `_git_source_state` hashes the CONTENTS of every untracked file, and the
# adversarial runner compares that fingerprint before and after building its
# binaries. A live build dir inside the repo therefore changes the fingerprint
# mid-build and the runner refuses with
#   "harness source, path dependency, toolchain, or build environment changed
#    while binaries were being built"
# — a correct fail-closed refusal that makes the gate unrunnable. (2026-09-19)
harness-target-probe/
harness-target/

# Scratch probe projects. Fix/review agents build one-off crates to reproduce a
# defect; those are evidence, not source, and one set (proj/r1/test_project)
# reached main on 2026-09-20 before this rule existed. Note the harness also
# hashes untracked files for its build fingerprint, so leaving scratch crates
# visible can make the adversarial gate refuse to run (see the harness-target
# entry above).
proj/
probes/
review-probes/
.pytest_cache/
